Title: The Call of the Wild

Credit: Written by

Author: Jack London

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER’S ESTATE – DAY

A sprawling, sun-dappled estate in the Santa Clara Valley. Amid wide lawns, shady poplar groves, and a cool swimming tank, BUCK—a large, majestic and confident dog—roams as if he is the undisputed king of his domain.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
In the warm light of Judge Miller’s place, Buck lived a life of comfort and unchallenged dominion. The estate was his kingdom—every tree, every patch of grass belonged to him.

EXT. ORCHARD NEAR A FLAG STATION – AFTERNOON

Buck wanders the orchard. Suddenly, two rough-handed MEN appear. One, wearing a threadbare red sweater, roughly fastens a rope around Buck’s neck.

MR. QUINCY
(gruffly)
“Wrap it up tight and don’t be slack.”

MR. KELLY
(continuing)
“Twist it and you’ll choke ‘m, plenty!”

The rope tightens. Buck’s eyes flash with indignation as the men haul him away toward a waiting wagon.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Unbeknownst to him, the world beyond comfort was calling—a brutal fate in the frozen north awaited, where men ruled by club and fang.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR – LATER

Buck lies confined in a cramped crate aboard a rattling train. The clamor of passing locomotives and the heavy thud of metal punctuate his confused growls.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Thrown into the jaws of a merciless transit, Buck’s noble spirit was battered by cruelty and shock as he was carted away from all he knew.

EXT. SNOWY TRAIL CAMP – DUSK

A rugged, icy camp buzzes with the harsh sounds of barking, clashing metal, and angry shouts. Buck, now on the trail, limps among other dogs whose eyes burn with wild desperation.

PERRAULT
(pointing at the dogs)
“A man with a club is the law here!”

FRANCOIS
(voice low, determined)
“Obedience or punishment. Out here, club and fang decide.”

Buck, bruised and wounded, meets the sneering glare of SPITZ—a lean, fierce rival. The two circle each other amid the chaotic chorus of snarls and barks.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
In the brutal wilderness, Buck learned quickly that only strength and cunning would allow him to survive. Each blow, each struggle, was a lesson in the law of the wild.

EXT. WIND-SWEPT TRAIL – DAY

Under a bleak sky and biting cold, Buck pulls a heavy sled along a narrow trail. His muscles strain as he devours every morsel of opportunity—snatching food, evading attacks, and growing fiercer with every step.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
Hunger and hardship honed his instincts. The comforts of the past faded, replaced by a raw determination to master the unforgiving north.

EXT. DAWSON CAMP – LATE MORNING

A rugged camp at the edge of civilization. Rough MEN handle paperwork as they unload Buck along with several other dogs. Among them, JOHN THORNTON—a calm, strong man with a kindly gaze—kneels and meets Buck’s heavy, searching eyes.

THORNTON
(softly, with warmth)
“You’re my good dog, Buck. Out here, only loyalty matters.”

Buck’s tail gives a tentative wag. His eyes reflect both pain and a dawning recognition—a promise of something new.

EXT. THORNTON’S CAMP – NIGHT

A small fire flickers amid snow. Thornton sits close to the glow as Buck rests by his side. The man pats his head roughly, and Buck leans into the touch, his ferocity tempered by a tenderness he has never known.

THORNTON
(gently, almost whispering)
“Rest now, friend. I’ll take care of you.”

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
In Thornton’s care, Buck experienced a love unlike any he had known—a bond that mended his battered pride, even as the wild call reached out from deep in the forest.

EXT. FOREST EDGE – DAWN

Buck stands at the fringe of a vast, ancient forest. The pale light of dawn reveals towering trees and a quiet promise of wild freedom. His ears prick as a distant, haunting howl rises among the pines.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
A sound echoed in his heart—a call from beyond the chains of civilization. The wild, primordial urge stirred within him once more.

EXT. OPEN FOREST – DAY

Buck bolts into the forest. He moves silently; his large frame melts into the shadows among ferns and towering evergreens. His eyes gleam with a newfound feral insight as he runs along hidden trails, past murmuring brooks and sun-dappled clearings.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
No longer bound by the memory of human kindness, Buck embraced his true self. The ties of the past loosened, and the spirit of the wolf—wild, free, and eternal—rose within him.

EXT. WILD CLEARING – SUNSET

On a windswept clearing under a radiant sunset, Buck pauses atop a rise. The wind dances through his shaggy coat. With a low, resonant howl that seems to carry the voice of countless generations, he proclaims his rebirth.

NARRATOR
(V.O.)
In one resounding moment, Buck answered the call of the wild—a call of survival, of ferocity, and of freedom. In that howl, the legacy of his forebears, the agony of his trials, and the hope of the untamed future converged.

Buck’s howl fades into the rustle of the forest as the screen slowly fades to black.

FADE OUT.
